**Read:** [Cryogenic Engineering — M. D. Atrey, IIT Bombay](../sources/Cryogenic-Engineering-Atrey.lingua#r_65F71B6VYE8297NF8ZN1D3S783), storage heat leaks; [Thermal performance of cryogenic storage tanks — Aasen and colleagues](../sources/Thermal-Performance-of-Cryogenic-Tanks-Aasen.lingua#r_2GPG0AVVS87R5ZY5Z5GVJEAEN7), the boil-off definition and case assumptions.

At maintained saturation conditions, with negligible sensible-energy storage and a defined vapor discharge, a useful leading estimate is ṁevap≈Q̇/hfg. It converts heat input into an equivalent vaporised mass rate. In a closed vessel, added heat can instead change internal energy, pressure and phase distribution, so this expression alone does not predict vented loss.

Take a hypothetical Q̇=10 W and constant hfg=200 kJ/kg. The equivalent rate is 5×10⁻⁵ kg/s, or 4.32 kg/day. Relative to an explicitly chosen 500 kg reference inventory, it is 0.864% per day. These assumed properties are not a rated performance for a particular gas or vessel. Specify whether a reported percentage uses initial, current, liquid-only or full-capacity mass.

If vapor is delivered as useful product, evaporation is not automatically a loss. Include recovered vapor, vented product, changes in stored mass and any recondensation separately. Warming the discharged gas also consumes heat; decide whether it occurs inside or outside the boundary used for the estimate.

**Work:** Reconcile heat and mass for storage at rest, gas withdrawal and liquid withdrawal. Explain why two vessels with the same measured mass loss may have different heat leaks and useful-output accounts.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
